unresolved.message:
@Dasha Andriyenko:
(1) 
Name: [on-screen string: Translation Beta]; 
Role: Dialog; 
Notes: "aria-describedby" to refer to the selected text (if possible)

(3-4) 
Name: [on-screen: From/To]; 
Role: <select> (<option> with the detected/target language is pre-selected)

(5) 
Name: Translated text (or similar, this would be only for screen readers, FYI @Emily Wachowiak for the proper label for this field); 
Role: Textarea;
Notes: "readonly" HTML attribute has to be provided, so a user could select text but cannot edit it

@Dasha Andriyenko
@Emily Wachowiak 

Can we decide on an official name for this? I would like to be consistent throughout filed bugs and especially throughout the code base with regard to variable names etc. 

I've heard this called the "call to action," the "quick link," the "shortcut" etc. 

I'd like to have a single name for this that we all agree on using.

@Dasha Andriyenko Would that be possible to make the disabled label text "Copy" a bit darker so it is more readable? 

There are no WCAG requirements, but it is likely to be informative to a user to know that copying would be possible (the icon is not needed to be readable to also support the visual disabled state indication, and the text could be 3:1 color contrast, it's 2.7:1 now).

[Added] And this would also be beneficial to the "Translate page" in disabled state. Again, the label text does not have to be bright, but just a bit more readable so the user would know what to expect in there, when these controls are enabled

@Ryan Casey something that is in the current model of Select Translation is that when a user clicks on the quick link. It get's replaced by the open panel. 

How does this work with the other tools though. 

Would we want the highlighter to go away for this moment as well?

@Anna Yeddi I'm not sure what's the best way of announcing the "translation in progress state"

Is it when the user opens the dialogue that they hear this?

idk, Ideally I'd like them to hear the translation state as one of the first things. But this would break the usual focus order.

